    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{1}{n-1}$ for $1\\le k\\le n-1$; $0$ otherwise, where $n\\ge2$ and $k$ is an integer.\n\nMultiply the conditional probabilities for one sequence, then multiply by the number of such sequences.",
      "worked": "Let $X$ be the number of baskets in the first $n$ shots. After the first two shots, there must be $k-1$ further successes and $n-k-1$ further failures.\n\nFor any particular order of these outcomes, the denominators of the conditional probabilities are $2,3,\\ldots,n-1$. The success numerators are $1,2,\\ldots,k-1$, and the failure numerators are $1,2,\\ldots,n-k-1$. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\Pr(\\text{one specified sequence})&=\\frac{(k-1)!(n-k-1)!}{(n-1)!}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThere are $\\binom{n-2}{k-1}$ possible positions for the further successes. Therefore\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\Pr(X=k)&=\\binom{n-2}{k-1}\\frac{(k-1)!(n-k-1)!}{(n-1)!}\\\\\n\\Pr(X=k)&=\\frac{(n-2)!}{(k-1)!(n-k-1)!}\\frac{(k-1)!(n-k-1)!}{(n-1)!}\\\\\n\\Pr(X=k)&=\\frac{1}{n-1}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThis holds for integers $1\\le k\\le n-1$, with $n\\ge2$. Outside this range the probability is $0$, since the first two shots already include one success and one failure.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 88
  },
